.SS "int32_t schultz_window_update (\fBschultz_window\fP * window, int32_t * out_quit)"

.PP
Advances the clock, draws one redraw, presents it, and reads input\&. The whole redraw in one call, in the only order that works:
.PP
.IP "1." 4
reclaim last redraw's scratch memory
.IP "2." 4
advance animations to the current time
.IP "3." 4
resolve styles, because painting reads only what that produces
.IP "4." 4
lay out whatever asked to be laid out
.IP "5." 4
read the dirty region, which bounds the next two steps
.IP "6." 4
paint the tree into a draw list, skipping what is outside it
.IP "7." 4
play that list through the rasterizer
.IP "8." 4
upload the dirty region and present
.IP "9." 4
turn platform input into events and route it
.PP
.PP
Input is read last so that what it changes is drawn by the next redraw, rather than half of it landing in this one\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwindow\fP The window\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIout_quit\fP Set to 1 when the user asked to close the window, 0 otherwise\&. May be NULL if the caller does not care\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or the first failure the redraw hit\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_window_request_close (\fBschultz_window\fP * window)"

.PP
Asks the redraw loop to stop after this redraw\&. What a Quit menu item calls\&. Without it a host has no way to end schultz_window_run of its own accord: the loop stops when the window is closed, and a machine showing one application on a bare screen has no window furniture to close it with\&.
.PP
Takes effect at the end of the current redraw, so a handler may call it and carry on doing whatever else it was doing\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwindow\fP The window\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_window_run (\fBschultz_window\fP * window, \fBschultz_window_update_fn\fP update, void * context, uint64_t max_frames)"

.PP
Runs the loop until the user closes the window\&. The loop a host would otherwise write, kept on this side so that a bound language pays one crossing a frame rather than nine\&.
.PP
\fBA host that cannot take an upcall drives the loop itself\&.\fP The update function is called once per turn, which is an upcall per frame; a host that would rather not be called at all keeps its own loop and calls schultz_window_update, which is one turn of this one\&. The nine crossings become one either way\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIwindow\fP The window\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIupdate\fP Called before each frame is drawn, or NULL for a still scene\&. 
.br
\fIcontext\fP Passed to update untouched\&. 
.br
\fImax_frames\fP Stop after drawing this many frames\&. Zero means run until the window is closed\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK when the loop ended because the window closed or the frame count ran out, or the failure that stopped it\&. 
.RE
.PP

